IPFS-IPLD FORMALIZATION

    Id(M) = Invariant(S) = H(E(S))

In this form IPFS is almost entirely a structure-first ontology. 
The network does not create identity; it merely mediates access 
to a pre-existing relational invariant.

Observations
------------
1. The strongest equation 

The deepest invariant is actually

    Id(M) = H(E(S))

because it strips away implementation details and exposes the
underlying principle:

    S = (B, R)
    R ⊆ B × B

Everything above that line can be swapped:

- different chunkers,
- different codecs,
- different hash functions,
- different transport protocols,

yet the architecture remains centered on the same idea:

    Identity = Invariant(S)

2. The network layer 

The final form implicitly induces a clean decomposition:

    IPFS = (Identity Layer) ⊕ (Discovery Layer) ⊕ (Transport Layer) ⊕ (Naming Layer)

with

    Identity Layer  = H(E(S))
    Discovery Layer = P
    Transport Layer = Bitswap / Graphsync
    Naming Layer    = φ

This separation is useful because it distinguishes what defines a 
thing from how a thing is found.

3. The recursive authentication theorem

Central theorem
---------------
Of everything in the specification, the statement

    Verify(CID(r))  ⟹  ∀ v ∈ Reach(r), Verify(CID(v))

is the closest thing to a canonical theorem.

Everything else follows from it:

- tamper evidence
- trustless retrieval
- partial verification
- authenticated subgraphs
- content addressing

Without that property the rest largely collapses.

4. Deduplication (quotient of identity)

The formulation

    B / ∼

is mathematically elegant because it shows that deduplication is 
not a storage optimization added afterward. It falls directly out 
of the identity relation.

Once

    b_i ∼ b_j

then

    [b_i] = [b_j]

Storage naturally operates on equivalence classes rather than on
instances. Deduplication is therefore a consequence of the identity
definition itself.

5. Ontological statement

Entire formalization reduced to a single line, as:

    Id(M) = H(E(S)),   S = (B, R)
    R ⊆ B × B

Everything else—

    P, Bitswap, Graphsync, φ

—exists only to answer the question

    “Where is S?”

while the identity equation answers

    “What is S?”

That distinction is what makes IPFS fundamentally different
from a location-addressed system.

6. Relational-style interpretation

Mapped into the preferred relational language:

    Structure:
        S = (B, R)

    Identity:
        I_d = H(E(S))

    Interaction:
        I = P + Bitswap + Graphsync

    Dynamics:
        D = φ(PID, t)

where

- Structure   = Merkle-linked content graph
- Interaction = discovery and exchange processes
- Dynamics    = mutable evolution through IPNS

ONTOLOGICAL CORE
----------------
S = (B, R)   where R subset of B x B is the Merkle-link relation

Id(M) = H(E(S))

Id(M) != Location(M)

Id(M) = Invariant(S)

Identity = H(E(S))

All networking machinery (provider relation, Bitswap,
Graphsync, IPNS) exists only to locate, transport, and
evolve instances of an identity already fixed by the hash
of the encoded relational structure.

TYPES AND FUNDAMENTALS
----------------------
F, M          : logical content / file / message
b, B          : block / set of blocks
chi           : chunking strategy (F -> {b_i})
G = (V, E)    : Merkle DAG / IPLD graph
N = (D, L)    : IPLD node (data segment, link list)
E             : deterministic codec / encoder
H             : cryptographic hash function
iota          : identity-generating map
CID           : Content Identifier
v             : CID version
codec         : multicodec identifier
multihash     : multihash structure (hash-id, digest)
Reach(r)      : set of nodes reachable from root r
P             : provider relation
N             : set of nodes / peers
W_u           : want-list of peer u
S_v           : have-set of peer v
L(u,v)        : bilateral ledger
sigma         : Graphsync selector (graph morphism)
PID           : PeerID / IPNS name
phi           : IPNS temporal map
T             : time domain
S = (B, R)    : relational structure (blocks + Merkle-link relation)
R             : Merkle-link relation (subset of B x B)

IDENTITY GENERATION VERSUS CID PACKAGING
-----------------------------------------
iota = H o E o G o chi

iota(F) = H(E(G(chi(F))))

CID = (v, codec(E), multihash(iota(F)))

MERKLE DAG AND RECURSIVE AUTHENTICATION
---------------------------------------
Reach(r) = { v in V | r leads-to v }

Verify(CID(r)) implies for all v in Reach(r): Verify(CID(v))
(provided all referenced child blocks are present)

The root CID authenticates the entire reachable subgraph
because each parent encoding embeds the CIDs of its children.

DHT PROVIDER RELATION
---------------------
P subset of H x N

(k, N) in P means "node N advertises content k"

P(k) = { N | (k, N) in P }

The DHT returns providers, never content.

BITSWAP AND GRAPHSYNC
---------------------
T(u,v) = W_u intersect S_v
(service further modulated by ledger L(u,v) or peer score)

sigma : G -> G'   with G' subset of G

Graphsync(G, sigma) = sigma(G)

DEDUPLICATION
-------------
b_i ~ b_j  iff  C(b_i) = C(b_j)

Storage operates over the quotient B / ~

Storage identity is the CID, not the raw hash.

IMMUTABLE VERSUS MUTABLE NAMING
-------------------------------
Immutable IPFS:   d/dt CID = 0

IPNS:             phi : PID x T -> CID
                  d/dt phi(PID, t) != 0 in general

The PeerID is constant; the pointed-to CID may change
via a signed record.

